  <h2>Shut it down</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Turn off the energy.</strong> Gas: turn the control knob to off or pilot, and close the gas valve on the supply line. Electric: switch off the water heater's breaker.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the cold water supply valve</strong> on the pipe going into the top of the tank. If it won't turn, shut off the main.</li>
    <li><strong>Let the water cool</strong> for a few hours if you need to drain it. Tank water can be hot enough to scald.</li>
    <li><strong>Drain the tank if it's leaking from the bottom.</strong> Attach a garden hose to the drain valve near the bottom, run it to a floor drain or outside, open a hot faucet upstairs to let air in, and open the drain valve.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where is it leaking from?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pipe connections on top</td><td>Loose fitting or corroded connector</td><td>Repairable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Temperature and pressure relief valve or its discharge pipe</td><td>Valve doing its job because pressure or temperature is too high, or a failing valve</td><td>Needs a plumber to diagnose. Never cap or plug this valve.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drain valve at the bottom</td><td>Valve not fully closed or worn out</td><td>Repairable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bottom of the tank or under the jacket</td><td>Tank corroded through</td><td>Replace the water heater</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Signs the tank is near the end</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rusty or discolored hot water, but not cold.</li>
    <li>Rumbling or popping from sediment buildup.</li>
    <li>Moisture or rust around the base.</li>
    <li>Age. Tank water heaters commonly last around 8 to 12 years. The manufacture date is usually coded into the serial number on the label.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limit the damage next time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Set the tank in a drain pan piped to a floor drain, especially in a finished basement or upstairs closet.</li>
    <li>Add a water leak sensor or an automatic shutoff valve.</li>
    <li>Flush sediment once a year, following the manufacturer's instructions.</li>
    <li>Ask a plumber to check the anode rod and whether you need a thermal expansion tank.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Insurance</h2>
  <p>Water damage from a sudden tank failure is usually covered. The water heater itself, and damage from a slow leak that went on for weeks, usually aren't.</p>
